  <model metaid="meta_enalapril_intestine" id="enalapril_intestine" name="Model for enalapril absorption in the small intestine" substanceUnits="mmole" timeUnits="min" volumeUnits="litre" areaUnits="m2" lengthUnits="metre" extentUnits="mmole">
    <notes>
      <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <h1>Model for enalapril absorption</h1>
        <p>The oral dose of enalapril (the maleate salt, dosed as enalapril) dissolves
and passes the stomach (<code>Ka_dis_ena</code>, replaced by the body model) into the
lumen of the small intestine, from where the fraction <code>F_ena_abs</code> is
absorbed into the portal plasma and the rest is excreted in the feces.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>56 +- 6 % of an oral dose of 14C-enalapril are recovered in the urine,
39 +- 5 % in the feces, i.e. about 60 % are absorbed {Ulm1982, Ball1987}</li>
          <li>the bioavailability of enalaprilat after enalapril is about 40 %, the
absorption of enalapril about 60-64 % {Hockings1986, Dickstein1987}</li>
          <li>food (a high fat breakfast) slows the absorption: Cmax of enalapril
falls to 0.54 at an AUC of 0.86 {Moffett2014}, the exposure to
enalaprilat and its urinary recovery do not change after a tablet with a
meal {Swanson1984}; the fed state slows the dissolution and the gastric
emptying (<code>fed</code>, <code>FED_f_dissolution</code>)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The content of this model has been carefully created in a manual research effort.</p>
